25	Use braces even for single statement after for loop or If condition

		Eg:	
			/* Dont code like this*/
			if( a==1)
				System.out.println("OK");
			
			/* This is fine*/
			if(a==1){
				System.out.println("OK");
			}	
			
			
27	Import order should be like below

			Java components (java.*)
			Javax components (javax.*)
			Apache components (org.apache.*)
			Open Source/Third Party components (com.* and org.*)
			Amicus Common components (com.amicus.commons.*)
			Amicus common framework components (com.amicus.commons.framework.*)
			Amicus common domain components (com.amicus.commons.domain.*)
			other Amicus components Feature Code (com.amicus.*)
			
28	Dont declare more than one attributes in a single line
		
		Eg	:	
				int i=0,j=20; // Dont use like this
				
				/* This is fine*/
				int i=0;
				int j=20;
				
				
30	Use one import directive per imported class

Example:
		/* This is not  fine*/
		<%@page import=”java.util.HashMap,java.util.Map”%>

		/* This if  fine*/
		<%@page import=”java.util.HashMap”%>
		<%@page import=”java.util.Map”%>
		
31	A package should contain minimum 5 classes. It should not exceed its maximum limit 50.


32.	Dont declare instance variable as public . This will create problems when other classes access the variable.


33	 Prefer declaring arrays as Type[] arrayName  rather than Type arrayName[]

	Eg	:	
			/*Avoid this*/
			String userList[]=new String[3];
			
			/* Use like this*/
			String[] userList=new String[3];
			
34	Avoid method level synchronization. Use block level synchronization.


35	Dont use '=='  operator while comparing two strings. Use equals() method.

36	When one java file contains more than one classes then put the public class in top.

37 	Declaring variables should be in following order

 			First the public class variables, then the protected, then package level (no access modifier), and then the private.
			
38	Wrapping the code
		
			a)	Break after a comma.
			
					var = someMethod1(longExpression1,
                					someMethod2(longExpression2,
                        								longExpression3)); 
										
			b)	Break before an operator.
			
			c) Following are two examples of breaking an arithmetic expression. The first is preferred, since the break occurs outside the parenthesized expression, which is at a higher level.

					longName1 = longName2 * (longName3 + longName4 - longName5)
						+ 4 * longname6; // PREFER
					
					longName1 = longName2 * (longName3 + longName4
							- longName5) + 4 * longname6; // AVOID 

			d)	Following are two examples of indenting method declarations. The first is the conventional case. The second would shift the second and third lines to the far right if it used conventional indentation, so instead it indents only 8 spaces.
					
					//CONVENTIONAL INDENTATION
					someMethod(int anArg, Object anotherArg, String yetAnotherArg,
						Object andStillAnother) {
					...
					}
					
					//INDENT 8 SPACES TO AVOID VERY DEEP INDENTS
					private static synchronized horkingLongMethodName(int anArg,
						Object anotherArg, String yetAnotherArg,
						Object andStillAnother) {
					...
					}
					
					
39	Avoid local declarations that hide declarations at higher levels. For example, do not declare the same variable name in an inner block:

		Eg	:
			int count;
			...
			myMethod() {
			if (condition) {
				int count = 0;     // AVOID!
				...
			}
			...
			}
